// The whole M3 heap surface, end to end: allocate, transfer ownership with
// `move`, release, and overwrite a live binding.
//
// Nothing reads the allocated memory -- M3 has no indexing yet, so a pointer is
// not yet a buffer -- and that is not the point. The program's job is to prove
// that the runtime's heap symbols link, that a real allocation succeeds and is
// released, and that every ownership transition the backend lowers is reachable
// in a real binary.
//
// The overwrite of `reused` is the interesting line: the checker knows the
// binding still owns a block there, so the backend releases it before storing
// the new one. `big` is a megabyte-sized allocation, which fails if `alloc`
// ever stopped scaling its element count by the element size.
fn main() {
    var buf = alloc(16);
    var other = move buf;
    dealloc(other);

    var reused = alloc(8);
    reused = alloc(32);
    dealloc(reused);

    var big = alloc(1000000);
    dealloc(big);

    println("memory ok");
}
